L(4, '1. Read async def, await and the event loop',
  'Distinguish a coroutine object from its result and run it under an owned event loop.',
  [('Coroutine function; coroutine object', 'async def defines a coroutine function. Calling it creates a coroutine object; that call alone does not run its body to completion.'),
   ('await; event loop', 'await waits for an awaitable result. If the operation suspends, the event loop can run other ready tasks. Awaiting an already ready operation need not yield control.'),
   ('asyncio.run; asyncio.sleep', 'run owns an event loop for a script entry point and closes it afterwards. sleep is a cooperative timer; time.sleep would block the thread running the loop.')],
  "Think of the event loop as a coordinator that resumes work when it can proceed. It does not turn every line into parallel execution. Ordinary Python statements inside a task run on that loop thread until execution suspends or returns.\n\nCalling an async function gives a coroutine, so remember to await it or schedule it. Forgetting that step can mean the work never runs and Python reports an unawaited-coroutine warning. The example keeps the body silent until main awaits it, then receives the returned number.\n\nUse asyncio.run once at the outer script boundary. In a notebook or another host that already owns a running event loop, use its supported top-level await instead of nesting asyncio.run. Save the supplied examples as scripts beside the Week 50 project files.",
  G('import asyncio\nasync def value():\n    print("Body started")\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.001)\n    return 7\nasync def main():\n    operation = value()\n    print("Created coroutine:", asyncio.iscoroutine(operation))\n    result = await operation\n    print("Returned result:", result)\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Write an async double function, await it, and print the returned number.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def double(number):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return number * 2\nasync def main():\n    print(await double(6))\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Call two coroutine functions in sequence and collect both results without creating unowned tasks.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def label(number):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return "item-" + str(number)\nasync def main():\n    first = await label(1)\n    second = await label(2)\n    print([first, second])\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  [('Does calling an async function immediately give its final returned value?', 'No; it creates a coroutine object', 'Always', 'Only when it has no arguments', 'The coroutine must be awaited or scheduled to execute.'),
   ('May asyncio.run be nested inside a currently running event loop in the same thread?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for short coroutines', 'The outer host already owns that loop; use its supported await interface.')],
  'Call an async function and treat the coroutine object as completed work.', 'Await the coroutine within an owned async lifetime.',
  'The creation message appears before the body message. Await returns 7, and the owner finishes without an unawaited coroutine.',
  syntax='async def main():\n    result = await operation()\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    asyncio.run(main())')

L(4, '2. Schedule sibling work with TaskGroup',
  'Make task ownership explicit and retrieve results after the group has finished.',
  [('Task; TaskGroup', 'A task schedules a coroutine on an event loop. TaskGroup owns a set of child tasks and waits for them when its async context exits; a non-cancellation failure cancels siblings and propagates.'),
   ('async with; task.result', 'async with awaits asynchronous context entry and exit. After a successful TaskGroup exit its tasks have finished, so result() retrieves their returned values.')],
  "Two consecutive await calls finish one operation before starting the next. To let independent operations overlap, create their tasks first. TaskGroup gives those tasks an enclosing lifetime so the parent cannot silently finish while its children remain unaccounted for.\n\nThe example schedules three timer-backed jobs and retains a mapping from input id to task. After the group exits successfully, it reconstructs input order. No assertion depends on which job completed first. A real application should preserve that same identity contract.\n\nTaskGroup is available from Python 3.11; this project uses 3.12 or 3.13. If a child raises a regular exception, the group cancels the remaining children, waits for them and raises the collected failure. Day 6 handles those failures deliberately. Adding tasks by itself does not bound memory or connection use.",
  G('import asyncio\nasync def work(number):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0.002)\n    return number * number\nasync def main():\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n        tasks = {i: group.create_task(work(i)) for i in range(3)}\n    print("All finished:", all(task.done() for task in tasks.values()))\n    print("Input-order results:", [tasks[i].result() for i in range(3)])\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Schedule two independent jobs together and retrieve their sum after group exit.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def amount(value):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return value\nasync def main():\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n        a = group.create_task(amount(12))\n        b = group.create_task(amount(8))\n    print(a.result() + b.result())\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Retain named input identities even when delays differ.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def load(name, delay):\n    await asyncio.sleep(delay)\n    return name.upper()\nasync def main():\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n        tasks = {name: group.create_task(load(name, delay)) for name, delay in [("first", 0.003), ("second", 0)]}\n    print({name: task.result() for name, task in tasks.items()})\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  [('What does successful TaskGroup exit establish?', 'All owned child tasks have finished', 'Every task ran on a separate CPU', 'There were no network waits', 'The group establishes lifetime ownership, not hardware parallelism.'),
   ('Do two consecutive await calls automatically start both operations together?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for strings', 'The second coroutine is normally called after the first await finishes.')],
  'Create background tasks and lose their handles or assume submission order is completion order.', 'Own the tasks and keep input identities alongside their results.',
  'All results are retrieved after successful group exit. Delays affect scheduling but do not alter the required output association.',
  syntax='async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n    first = group.create_task(work_a())\n    second = group.create_task(work_b())\n# Both finished here if the group exited successfully.')

L(4, '3. Keep blocking calls off the loop thread',
  'Use cooperative operations or explicitly offload finite synchronous I/O.',
  [('asyncio.to_thread', 'Runs a synchronous function in a thread and returns an awaitable result. It helps integrate blocking I/O; it is not a general pure-Python CPU parallelism promise on GIL-enabled CPython.'),
   ('asyncio.Event', 'A loop-owned signal: await event.wait() suspends until another task calls set(). It is different from threading.Event and is not a cross-thread synchronization primitive.')],
  "An async function can still block its event loop. A long pure Python loop, time.sleep, or a synchronous HTTP call runs normally if called directly inside it. While that code occupies the loop thread, other tasks cannot advance there. Merely adding async to the function header changes none of this.\n\nPrefer an async library for frequent I/O. For a finite legacy blocking call, await asyncio.to_thread(callable, arguments) lets a thread perform it while the loop remains available. Do not call the function first and pass its returned value by mistake. The example records both a thread-offloaded result and a cooperative heartbeat.\n\nCancellation of the awaiting task does not kill the blocking function already running in its thread. Keep such calls finite and give their underlying I/O appropriate timeouts. For substantial pure Python computation, use the explicit process boundary taught later; copying data and starting workers still cost time.",
  G('import asyncio\nimport time\ndef legacy_read():\n    time.sleep(0.01)\n    return "local record"\nasync def heartbeat(events):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    events.append("loop ran")\nasync def main():\n    events = []\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n        read = group.create_task(asyncio.to_thread(legacy_read))\n        group.create_task(heartbeat(events))\n    print("Result:", read.result())\n    print("Heartbeat completed:", events == ["loop ran"])\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Offload a small real synchronous file read and await the returned text.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom pathlib import Path\nasync def main():\n    path = Path("owned-example.txt")\n    path.write_text("hello", encoding="utf-8")\n    text = await asyncio.to_thread(path.read_text, encoding="utf-8")\n    print(text)\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Coordinate two tasks with asyncio.Event rather than blocking the loop with a threading wait.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    ready = asyncio.Event()\n    async def reader():\n        await ready.wait()\n        return "signal received"\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n        task = group.create_task(reader())\n        ready.set()\n    print(task.result())\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  [('Does async def prevent a direct time.sleep call from blocking its loop thread?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when a task group exists', 'Synchronous work keeps its ordinary blocking behavior.'),
   ('Which call correctly offloads a synchronous function?', 'await asyncio.to_thread(read_file, path)', 'await asyncio.to_thread(read_file(path))', 'read_file(path)', 'Pass the callable and its arguments; do not execute it first on the loop.')],
  'Place blocking I/O in async def and assume other tasks can run while it blocks.', 'Use an async operation or offload the finite blocking call with explicit ownership.',
  'The example verifies both results, not an exact task completion order or a guaranteed speedup.',
  syntax='value = await asyncio.to_thread(blocking_function, argument)')

L(4, '4. Reuse one AsyncClient for owned HTTP work',
  'Await transport operations, validate the response and close the client scope.',
  [('httpx.AsyncClient', 'HTTPX async client: an async context manager that owns HTTP connections. Reuse one client for a logical collection batch instead of constructing one per item.'),
   ('raise_for_status; response.json', 'raise_for_status exposes an unsuccessful HTTP status. With ordinary await client.get the response body is loaded; json() parses it synchronously and is not itself awaited.'),
   ('trust_env=False; local_sources', 'This fixture ignores proxy/environment settings and binds only to 127.0.0.1. local_sources is the supplied context manager for fictional local HTTP responses and joined server cleanup.')],
  "The learning server runs on your own computer and chooses an available port. Its small response contains id and value. HTTPX still uses a real socket, so the exercise can reveal HTTP status and decoding errors without depending on a public website or an account.\n\nOpen one AsyncClient around related requests and await its get method. Check status before interpreting JSON, then check the expected data shape. A 200 response alone is not proof that a payload matches your contract. Our collection helper adds strict id, key and integer checks.\n\nHere the ordinary get call reads the whole tiny response body before returning. Streaming arbitrary large bodies needs a separate design. The async client closes before the outer owned fixture stops; keep these scopes visible rather than leaving resource lifetime to interpreter shutdown.",
  G('import asyncio\nimport httpx\nfrom sources import local_sources\nasync def main(base):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=1, trust_env=False) as client:\n        response = await client.get(base + "/item/2")\n        response.raise_for_status()\n        payload = response.json()\n        assert set(payload) == {"id", "value"}\n        assert payload["id"] == 2\n        print(payload)\nwith local_sources(delay=0.002) as (base, state):\n    asyncio.run(main(base))\nprint("Active handlers after cleanup:", state.snapshot()["active"])'),
  'Use the same client for two sequential awaited requests and verify their requested identities.',
  G('import asyncio\nimport httpx\nfrom sources import local_sources\nasync def main(base):\n    rows = []\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=1, trust_env=False) as client:\n        for identifier in [0, 1]:\n            response = await client.get(f"{base}/item/{identifier}")\n            response.raise_for_status()\n            row = response.json()\n            assert row["id"] == identifier\n            rows.append(row)\n    print(rows)\nwith local_sources(delay=0.001) as (base, _):\n    asyncio.run(main(base))'),
  'Detect a fixture HTTP 503 explicitly without reporting a successful payload.',
  G('import asyncio\nimport httpx\nfrom sources import local_sources\nasync def main(base):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=1, trust_env=False) as client:\n        response = await client.get(base + "/item/1")\n        try:\n            response.raise_for_status()\n        except httpx.HTTPStatusError:\n            print("Request failed:", response.status_code)\nwith local_sources(delay=0, fail_ids={1}) as (base, _):\n    asyncio.run(main(base))'),
  [('Which operation is awaited for a normal buffered HTTPX async request?', 'client.get(url)', 'response.json()', 'response.status_code', 'The network operation is async; decoding this already loaded tiny body is synchronous.'),
   ('Does HTTP 200 alone prove the payload id matches the requested job?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for JSON', 'Validate the application data contract separately from HTTP status.')],
  'Create a client for every item or treat every received response as valid data.', 'Reuse an owned client and check HTTP status plus payload identity and shape.',
  'The fixture returns id 2 and value 39. After both scopes exit, the server has no active owned handler.',
  syntax='async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=1, trust_env=False) as client:\n    response = await client.get(url)\n    response.raise_for_status()\n    payload = response.json()')

L(4, '5. Bound tasks, queued jobs and connections separately',
  'Explain what each resource limit controls and use a fixed worker group.',
  [('asyncio.Semaphore', 'An async permit counter that can limit a protected active section. Creating a million tasks first still creates a million tasks, even if they all wait for a small semaphore.'),
   ('asyncio.Queue(maxsize); backpressure', 'A bounded async queue suspends put when full. A slow downstream stage therefore makes the producer wait instead of accumulating unlimited queued work.'),
   ('fetch_many; make_jobs', 'Supplied helpers: make_jobs builds a finite owned-fixture manifest. fetch_many uses one producer, fixed consumers, a bounded job queue and a shared AsyncClient, returning records/errors/progress/limits.')],
  "Worker count, connection count and queued-job count answer different questions. A pool of four connections does not prevent you from allocating thousands of task objects. A semaphore limits entry to a section, but does not automatically limit the number of waiting tasks.\n\nOur helper schedules one producer and a fixed number of consumers. The producer awaits a bounded queue. Each consumer handles one request at a time and the shared client also has a connection limit. After production, one sentinel per consumer lets every worker finish normally.\n\nThis bounds work in flight, not all memory. The supplied helper accepts a finite manifest and returns stored results, so both still grow with the total number of jobs. A streaming source and durable sink would need additional contracts. The example exposes the configured limits and checks one progress event per finished request.",
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nprogress = []\nwith local_sources(delay=0.003) as (base, _):\n    result = asyncio.run(fetch_many(make_jobs(base, 6), concurrency=2, progress=lambda done, total: progress.append((done, total))))\nprint("Records:", len(result["records"]))\nprint("Errors:", result["errors"])\nprint("Limits:", result["limits"])\nprint("Counted once each:", progress == [(i, 6) for i in range(1, 7)])'),
  'Show that a bounded async queue makes a producer wait while its only slot is full.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    queue = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=1)\n    await queue.put("first")\n    started = asyncio.Event()\n    async def producer():\n        started.set()\n        await queue.put("second")\n    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n        task = group.create_task(producer())\n        await started.wait()\n        print("Producer waiting:", not task.done())\n        print(await queue.get())\n        queue.task_done()\n    print(await queue.get())\n    queue.task_done()\n    await queue.join()\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Handle an empty manifest without inventing progress or losing worker shutdown.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0) as (base, _):\n    result = asyncio.run(fetch_many(make_jobs(base, 0), concurrency=2))\nprint(result["records"], result["errors"], result["progress"])'),
  [('Does a semaphore of four prevent creation of a million waiting tasks?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only when used with HTTPX', 'Task creation and admission to a protected section are separate limits.'),
   ('Do bounded queues alone make this finite-manifest collector use constant total memory?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with two workers', 'The input manifest and returned results are also retained.')],
  'Create one task per item in an arbitrarily large source and call that bounded because connections are capped.', 'Use a fixed worker count, bounded queues and declared result-storage behavior.',
  'The queue exercise uses an explicit started signal; it does not guess whether a producer ran by sleeping for an arbitrary duration.',
  syntax='queue = asyncio.Queue(maxsize=capacity)\nawait queue.put(job)  # waits when full\njob = await queue.get()  # waits when empty')

L(4, '6. Fetch 100 local URLs and check complete results',
  'Compare bounded async I/O with a sequential reference using the same 100 jobs.',
  [('Normalization before comparison', 'fetch_many returns id/value payloads; collect_sync returns id/score rows. Apply the same declared scoring operation before comparing their complete outputs.'),
   ('Complete-request benchmark', 'Measure the whole collector call, including consumption and client cleanup, and reject missing/error results. This is an owned loopback fixture, not public-network throughput.')],
  "This is the first full 100-URL exercise. Each URL belongs to the local learning server and has a stable input id. Four async consumers overlap waiting without scheduling 100 independent unbounded workers.\n\nUse rounds=0 in both comparisons so the focus is transport and validation, not substantial CPU scoring. Normalize the returned raw async payloads with that same operation inside the timed scope. Require zero errors and complete equality with the sequential reference before interpreting the elapsed times.\n\nYour numbers will vary with the machine and scheduler. The observed ratio is sequential seconds divided by async seconds; below one means async took longer. This one trial is an exercise in measurement, not a performance guarantee. Day 6 repeats full mixed-work experiments and retains every timing sample.",
  G('import asyncio\nfrom time import perf_counter\nfrom collector import collect_sync, fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nfrom workers import score_payload\nwith local_sources(delay=0.005) as (base, _):\n    jobs = make_jobs(base, 100)\n    start = perf_counter()\n    expected = collect_sync(jobs, rounds=0)\n    sequential = perf_counter() - start\n    start = perf_counter()\n    result = asyncio.run(fetch_many(jobs, concurrency=4))\n    actual = [score_payload(row, 0) for row in result["records"]]\n    asynchronous = perf_counter() - start\nassert not result["errors"] and actual == expected and len(actual) == 100\nprint("Equal complete results:", len(actual))\nprint("Sequential / async seconds:", round(sequential, 4), round(asynchronous, 4))\nprint("Observed ratio:", round(sequential / asynchronous, 2))\nprint("Progress:", result["progress"])'),
  'Verify every requested id appears exactly once in a smaller async run.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0.001) as (base, _):\n    result = asyncio.run(fetch_many(make_jobs(base, 10), concurrency=2))\nids = [row["id"] for row in result["records"]]\nassert not result["errors"] and ids == list(range(10))\nprint("All ten ids present exactly once:", len(set(ids)) == 10)'),
  'Repeat a small collection twice and compare actual data without asserting equal elapsed times.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0.001) as (base, _):\n    jobs = make_jobs(base, 4)\n    first = asyncio.run(fetch_many(jobs, concurrency=2))\n    second = asyncio.run(fetch_many(jobs, concurrency=2))\nassert not first["errors"] and not second["errors"]\nprint("Same complete records:", first["records"] == second["records"])'),
  [('When is a timing comparison acceptable here?', 'After checking equal complete output and zero errors', 'Whenever async reports a smaller number', 'After scheduling the requests only', 'A failed or incomplete workload is not a valid faster implementation.'),
   ('Are these timings a promise for any public website?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for 100 URLs', 'Local synthetic delay and tiny payloads define a deliberately limited workload.')],
  'Compare raw payloads with scored rows or omit normalization from the timed async scope.', 'Normalize identically, consume fully, then compare the declared complete work.',
  'All 100 requests are actual local HTTP operations. Their equal sorted results are checked before the observation is reported.',
  syntax='start = perf_counter()\ncomplete_results = run_and_normalize_all_work()\nelapsed = perf_counter() - start\nassert complete_results == reference',
  diagram=('Bounded async HTTP collection', ['Finite manifest feeds one producer', 'Bounded queue makes the producer wait when full', 'Fixed async workers await owned HTTP requests', 'One shared client caps active connections', 'Owner checks errors and restores result identity'], 'These bounds control in-flight work. The finite manifest and final stored results still grow with job count; async scheduling does not create CPU capacity.'))

L(5, '1. Separate expected request failures from bugs',
  'Keep expected failures visible without silently converting programming errors into data.',
  [('Expected error record', 'A declared request or validation failure becomes an id/error record. It is a completed attempt, not a successful payload.'),
   ('ExceptionGroup; except*', 'A task group can raise several child failures together. except* handles matching exceptions within that group while leaving other failure types to propagate.')],
  "A batch collector must define its error policy. Our async helpers continue after expected HTTP status, transport, deadline and tiny-payload validation errors. They record the failed id so a missing result cannot masquerade as successful complete output.\n\nThat catch is deliberately placed around fetching and decoding. A broken progress callback, a CPU worker bug or a failure in coordination is outside it. Such failures must escape and cancel siblings through TaskGroup; catching every Exception around the whole worker would hide broken program logic.\n\nThe example requests five fixture records with one HTTP failure and one malformed body. Three successes plus two explicit failures account for all five attempts. The challenge injects a callback bug and handles its RuntimeError subgroup for demonstration, rather than reporting that bug as a successful collection.",
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0.002, fail_ids={1}, malformed_ids={3}) as (base, _):\n    result = asyncio.run(fetch_many(make_jobs(base, 5), concurrency=2))\nprint("Successful ids:", [row["id"] for row in result["records"]])\nprint("Failed ids:", [row["id"] for row in result["errors"]])\nprint("All attempts accounted for:", len(result["records"]) + len(result["errors"]) == 5)\nprint("Progress:", result["progress"])'),
  'Separate successful and failed ids and verify their sets are disjoint and complete.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0, fail_ids={2}) as (base, _):\n    result = asyncio.run(fetch_many(make_jobs(base, 4), concurrency=2))\nsuccess = {row["id"] for row in result["records"]}\nfailed = {row["id"] for row in result["errors"]}\nprint("Disjoint:", success.isdisjoint(failed))\nprint("Complete accounting:", success | failed == set(range(4)))'),
  'Demonstrate that an unexpected progress callback bug propagates from the task group.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\ndef broken(done, total):\n    raise RuntimeError("callback bug")\nwith local_sources(delay=0.001) as (base, _):\n    try:\n        asyncio.run(fetch_many(make_jobs(base, 4), concurrency=2, progress=broken))\n    except* RuntimeError:\n        print("Unexpected callback bug propagated")'),
  [('Does completed=5 mean all five requests succeeded?', 'No; progress counts completed attempts', 'Always', 'Only for JSON', 'Expected failed attempts also advance the completed count.'),
   ('Should a callback programming bug become a normal successful collection?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only if another request succeeded', 'Unexpected failures must remain visible and trigger owned cleanup.')],
  'Catch every error around all worker behavior and substitute an empty successful result.', 'Catch declared request failures narrowly and propagate unexpected bugs.',
  'Expected failures retain their ids. The injected programming bug is observed through exception-group handling.',
  syntax='try:\n    payload = await fetch_and_validate(job)\nexcept expected_request_errors as exc:\n    errors.append({"id": job.id, "error": type(exc).__name__})')

L(5, '2. Give deadlines a precise scope',
  'Distinguish transport phase timeouts from total request and end-to-end deadlines.',
  [('asyncio.timeout', 'An async context that cancels the current task when its time allowance expires and converts its own cancellation into TimeoutError outside the context.'),
   ('HTTPX phase timeouts', 'HTTPX can bound connect, read, write and connection-pool waits separately. A phase limit is not automatically a single total elapsed budget for every phase combined.'),
   ('Queue wait versus request scope', 'Our total request deadline begins when a worker starts fetching. Time spent waiting in the job queue and later CPU work is outside it.')],
  "A number named timeout is incomplete documentation. State when it starts, what work it covers and what happens after it expires. Our helper wraps the actual request and validation in asyncio.timeout and also configures HTTPX transport limits.\n\nThe slow fixture response waits 0.2 seconds while ordinary responses wait 0.002. A short request budget makes the slow request fail visibly; the exact exception class may depend on which deadline fires first. This example checks failed ids and retained successes, not machine-specific timing or one exact transport exception.\n\nA client timeout does not stop an already executing server handler. The owned local fixture closes only after its finite handlers finish. If you need a whole-batch deadline, wrap the whole operation separately and still account for cancellation cleanup. Completion of cleanup can extend beyond the deadline itself.",
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import fetch_many, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0.002, slow_ids={0}) as (base, state):\n    result = asyncio.run(fetch_many(make_jobs(base, 3), concurrency=2, timeout=0.05))\nprint("Failed ids:", [row["id"] for row in result["errors"]])\nprint("Successful ids:", [row["id"] for row in result["records"]])\nprint("Owned handlers after cleanup:", state.snapshot()["active"])'),
  'Catch TimeoutError outside an asyncio.timeout context around a never-set event.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    waiting = asyncio.Event()\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(0.01):\n            await waiting.wait()\n    except TimeoutError:\n        print("Request-scope deadline expired")\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Verify finally cleanup executes when a deadline cancels the waiting operation.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    cleaned = []\n    async def operation():\n        try:\n            await asyncio.Event().wait()\n        finally:\n            cleaned.append("released")\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.timeout(0.01):\n            await operation()\n    except TimeoutError:\n        print("Deadline reported")\n    print("Cleanup observed:", cleaned == ["released"])\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  [('Does this helper include time waiting in its job queue in each request deadline?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only with four workers', 'The request timeout scope starts inside the fetch worker.'),
   ('Does timing out the client forcibly kill the server handler?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only on localhost', 'The client and server own separate execution lifetimes.')],
  'Promise a total batch deadline from a per-phase timeout or assume timeout forcibly stops remote work.', 'Document the clock scope, failure result and cleanup obligations.',
  'The lesson distinguishes failing to receive a result in time from forcibly ending every underlying activity.',
  syntax='try:\n    async with asyncio.timeout(seconds):\n        result = await operation()\nexcept TimeoutError:\n    handle_declared_timeout()')

L(5, '3. Propagate cancellation after cleanup',
  'Use explicit signals to test cancellation and let the owner observe CancelledError.',
  [('Task.cancel; CancelledError', 'cancel requests cooperative cancellation. A coroutine normally receives CancelledError at an opportunity to suspend; it is not an immediate forceful stop of arbitrary code.'),
   ('finally on cancellation', 'Release owned resources in finally. If you catch CancelledError to perform cleanup, normally re-raise it so the owner and structured concurrency can recognize cancellation.')],
  "Cancellation is a request that travels through an awaitable lifetime. If code never yields and occupies the loop with blocking work, the loop cannot promptly deliver it. A running synchronous thread or process call may continue even after the task awaiting its result is cancelled.\n\nThe example uses an Event to prove the child has started, then cancels it and awaits its termination. The finally block records cleanup before the owner sees CancelledError. This avoids a timing guess such as sleeping a few milliseconds and hoping the child was already active.\n\nThe same ownership matters when one TaskGroup child fails. Its waiting sibling is cancelled and gets a chance to clean up before the group raises. Do not swallow cancellation merely to make a progress counter look complete; cancelled work is a distinct outcome.",
  G('import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    started = asyncio.Event()\n    cleaned = asyncio.Event()\n    async def worker():\n        started.set()\n        try:\n            await asyncio.Event().wait()\n        finally:\n            cleaned.set()\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker())\n    await started.wait()\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        print("Owner observed cancellation")\n    print("Cleanup finished:", cleaned.is_set())\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Catch cancellation to record a cleanup fact and re-raise it to the owner.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    started = asyncio.Event()\n    events = []\n    async def worker():\n        started.set()\n        try:\n            await asyncio.Event().wait()\n        except asyncio.CancelledError:\n            events.append("cleanup")\n            raise\n    task = asyncio.create_task(worker())\n    await started.wait()\n    task.cancel()\n    try:\n        await task\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:\n        events.append("owner notified")\n    print(events)\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Make one TaskGroup child fail only after its waiting sibling has started, then verify sibling cleanup.',
  G('import asyncio\nasync def main():\n    ready = asyncio.Event()\n    cleaned = []\n    async def waiting():\n        ready.set()\n        try:\n            await asyncio.Event().wait()\n        finally:\n            cleaned.append(True)\n    async def broken():\n        await ready.wait()\n        raise ValueError("deliberate child failure")\n    try:\n        async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as group:\n            group.create_task(waiting())\n            group.create_task(broken())\n    except* ValueError:\n        print("Group failure observed")\n    print("Sibling cleaned:", cleaned == [True])\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  [('After catching CancelledError for cleanup, what should normal cancellation handling do?', 'Re-raise it', 'Always return a fake success', 'Delete every result', 'The owner needs to observe cancellation; structured concurrency relies on it.'),
   ('Does task.cancel forcibly stop a process function that is already running?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for small jobs', 'Cancellation of an awaiting task is distinct from hard process termination.')],
  'Swallow cancellation and assume a running external call was killed.', 'Clean up owned resources, propagate cancellation and account for continuing finite work.',
  'Explicit readiness signals make the cancellation exercises deterministic without asserting exact scheduler timing.',
  syntax='try:\n    await operation()\nfinally:\n    release_owned_resource()')

L(5, '4. Move CPU work across an explicit process boundary',
  'Await an importable process worker without running its Python loop on the event loop thread.',
  [('get_running_loop; run_in_executor', 'get_running_loop returns the loop that owns the current coroutine. loop.run_in_executor(pool, function, args...) submits synchronous work and returns an awaitable future.'),
   ('Explicit process pool ownership', 'Use a spawn ProcessPoolExecutor and an importable function for CPU work. Close the pool in finally; running calls are finite and may need time to finish after cancellation.'),
   ('Offloaded pool shutdown', 'Pool shutdown is synchronous and may wait. await asyncio.to_thread(pool.shutdown, wait=True, cancel_futures=True) keeps that join off the loop thread.')],
  "The event loop should coordinate the CPU stage, not execute a long arithmetic loop itself. Submit the supplied module-level score_payload function to an explicit process pool and await its result. The argument and result cross a serialization boundary, so keep them small and ordinary.\n\nThe default executor used by to_thread is a thread pool. For this lesson's pure Python arithmetic on ordinary GIL-enabled CPython, choose processes when testing parallel computation. That still does not guarantee a speedup: spawn, import, transfer and joining can outweigh small jobs.\n\nProtect script entry with the main guard. Each CPU consumer in the final project awaits its outstanding job before submitting another, which bounds process submissions. In cleanup, queued calls may be cancelled but already running calls must finish; waiting through a thread keeps the event loop responsive while the owner joins them.",
  G('import asyncio\nimport multiprocessing as mp\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nfrom workers import score_payload\nasync def main():\n    pool = ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=1, mp_context=mp.get_context("spawn"))\n    try:\n        loop = asyncio.get_running_loop()\n        payload = {"id": 2, "value": 39}\n        result = await loop.run_in_executor(pool, score_payload, payload, 1000)\n        print("Same computation:", result == score_payload(payload, 1000))\n        print(result)\n    finally:\n        await asyncio.to_thread(pool.shutdown, wait=True, cancel_futures=True)\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Await a small importable square worker through an explicitly owned process pool.',
  G('import asyncio\nimport multiprocessing as mp\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nfrom workers import square\nasync def main():\n    pool = ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=1, mp_context=mp.get_context("spawn"))\n    try:\n        print(await asyncio.get_running_loop().run_in_executor(pool, square, 9))\n    finally:\n        await asyncio.to_thread(pool.shutdown, wait=True, cancel_futures=True)\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  'Propagate an ordinary process-worker ValueError through its awaitable future and still shut down the pool.',
  G('import asyncio\nimport multiprocessing as mp\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\nfrom workers import fail_job\nasync def main():\n    pool = ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=1, mp_context=mp.get_context("spawn"))\n    try:\n        try:\n            await asyncio.get_running_loop().run_in_executor(pool, fail_job, "worker failed")\n        except ValueError as exc:\n            print(str(exc))\n    finally:\n        await asyncio.to_thread(pool.shutdown, wait=True, cancel_futures=True)\nasyncio.run(main())'),
  [('Does asyncio.to_thread automatically select a ProcessPoolExecutor?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for arithmetic', 'It offloads to a thread; the CPU process boundary here is explicit.'),
   ('Why await one process call before a CPU consumer takes its next item?', 'To bound outstanding submitted work', 'To disable serialization', 'To guarantee a speedup', 'A bounded queue is insufficient if consumers immediately flood an unbounded submission backlog.')],
  'Run substantial pure Python scoring directly inside async def or flood the process executor with all jobs.', 'Submit compact jobs through a bounded awaited process stage and own shutdown.',
  'The saved-script guard and supplied importable worker are part of the runnable example. Output equality is checked without a performance promise.',
  syntax='result = await loop.run_in_executor(process_pool, importable_worker, payload)')

L(5, '5. Connect bounded stages and count progress honestly',
  'Integrate HTTP, CPU scoring, errors and backpressure under one owner.',
  [('collect_pipeline', 'Supplied async coordinator: bounded job and payload queues connect fixed HTTP workers to fixed CPU consumers using a spawn pool. It returns successful rows, explicit expected errors, progress and limits.'),
   ('Stage completion markers', 'A coordinator waits for all fetch workers to finish before sending one sentinel per CPU consumer. Sending stop markers too early could strand valid payloads.'),
   ('Progress callback contract', 'A quick synchronous callback runs on the event loop for each successful score or expected request failure. done/total counts completed attempts; errors remain separately visible.')],
  "Follow one job: the producer queues its id and URL, a fetch worker validates the local response, the payload enters a bounded second queue, and a CPU consumer awaits a scored result. Only then does a successful job count as completed. An expected failed fetch counts once without entering the CPU stage.\n\nIf CPU scoring is slower, the payload queue fills and fetch workers wait. This in turn fills the job queue and slows the producer. That is backpressure. It limits queued work rather than pretending every stage has infinite capacity. The finite manifest and final result/error lists still use memory proportional to the workload.\n\nThe example uses small queue capacity to exercise this coordination. Progress is monotonic but does not imply jobs finish in id order. Keep the callback fast; blocking UI or file work there would block the loop. An unexpected callback or scoring error aborts the group and triggers owned cleanup instead of producing a fake final report.",
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import collect_pipeline, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nupdates = []\nwith local_sources(delay=0.002, fail_ids={1}) as (base, _):\n    result = asyncio.run(collect_pipeline(make_jobs(base, 6), concurrency=2, cpu_workers=1, rounds=1000, queue_capacity=1, progress=lambda done, total: updates.append((done, total))))\nprint("Successful ids:", [row["id"] for row in result["records"]])\nprint("Failed ids:", [row["id"] for row in result["errors"]])\nprint("Progress:", result["progress"])\nprint("Counted once:", updates == [(i, 6) for i in range(1, 7)])\nprint("Limits:", result["limits"])'),
  'Compare the full mixed pipeline with a complete sequential reference for the same rounds and jobs.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import collect_pipeline, collect_sync, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0.001) as (base, _):\n    jobs = make_jobs(base, 5)\n    expected = collect_sync(jobs, rounds=100)\n    result = asyncio.run(collect_pipeline(jobs, concurrency=2, cpu_workers=1, rounds=100, queue_capacity=1))\nassert not result["errors"]\nprint("Complete equal scored output:", result["records"] == expected)'),
  'Change CPU and fetch worker counts and verify data stays equal rather than assuming timing improves.',
  G('import asyncio\nfrom collector import collect_pipeline, make_jobs\nfrom sources import local_sources\nwith local_sources(delay=0.001) as (base, _):\n    jobs = make_jobs(base, 4)\n    first = asyncio.run(collect_pipeline(jobs, concurrency=1, cpu_workers=1, rounds=100, queue_capacity=1))\n    second = asyncio.run(collect_pipeline(jobs, concurrency=2, cpu_workers=2, rounds=100, queue_capacity=2))\nassert not first["errors"] and not second["errors"]\nprint("Different limits, same results:", first["records"] == second["records"])'),
  [('When does a successful pipeline job advance completed?', 'After its CPU result is collected', 'As soon as its URL is queued', 'Only when the whole batch exits', 'Completed means the declared job outcome is available, not merely scheduled.'),
   ('What happens when the payload queue fills?', 'Fetch workers wait and pressure can reach the producer', 'CPU capacity becomes infinite', 'The queue deletes old payloads', 'Awaited bounded puts propagate downstream slowness upstream.')],
  'Count submitted jobs as finished or emit CPU stop markers before all fetchers have produced their payloads.', 'Count actual outcomes once and close each downstream stage after its producers finish.',
  'Five scored successes and one explicit failure account for all six attempts. Different limits do not change the required data.',
  syntax='producer -> bounded jobs -> HTTP workers -> bounded payloads -> awaited CPU workers -> outcomes')

L(5, '6. Milestone: a measured bounded collector',
  'Run the complete experiment, reconstruct its artifacts and explain observed performance limits.',
  [('write_report; run_experiment', 'Supplied lab helpers run equal complete workloads, rotate method order, preserve raw timing samples and save results.csv plus report.json. The report includes environment, workload, bounds and explicit limitations.'),
   ('Reconstructable result checksum', 'The report stores count and the sum of scores so the CSV can reproduce them. This checksum checks artifact consistency; it is not a cryptographic authenticity guarantee.'),
   ('Observed median chart', 'Bars show measured complete-collector elapsed seconds; lower is faster for this declared workload. A few trials are descriptive evidence, not confidence intervals or production predictions.')],
  "Finish by running the actual project. The default CLI uses 100 owned local URLs and three repetitions of sequential, four-thread and async-plus-two-process collection. Each method performs the same validation and scoring. Any missing result, unexpected error or unequal complete output invalidates the benchmark.\n\nThe plotted example uses two repetitions to keep the lesson run small. It includes client work, CPU scoring, process startup, data transfer and shutdown; the shared fixture lifetime is outside the timed collector calls. A separate CPU-only experiment compares sequential, threaded and process execution with identical pure Python arithmetic. Small process jobs can be slower, and the report keeps that result.\n\nBefore calling the milestone complete, read the project tests, reconstruct CSV count/checksum, inspect failures and cancellation, then explain which workload stage limits progress. Change one limit at a time and rerun equal work. Study Hub hosts these lessons; the Python collector runs as your local practice project. The chart and checked outputs are embedded for offline reading.",
  G('from lab import write_report\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nreport = write_report("artifacts", count=100, repeats=2)\nprint("Equal complete results:", report["all_results_equal"], report["result_count"])\nprint("Score checksum:", report["result_checksum"])\nmedians = report["collection_median_seconds"]\nprint("Collector median seconds:", {k: round(v, 3) for k, v in medians.items()})\nprint("CPU median seconds:", {k: round(v, 3) for k, v in report["cpu_experiment"]["median_seconds"].items()})\nprint("Final progress:", report["final_pipeline_progress"])\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.3, 4.6))\nvalues = list(medians.values())\nbars = ax.bar(["Sequential", "4 threads", "Async +\\n2 processes"], values, color=["#247ba0", "#568b48", "#cf6e27"])\nax.bar_label(bars, labels=[f"{value:.3f}s" for value in values], padding=5, fontsize=10)\nax.set_ylim(0, max(values) * 1.22)\nax.set_ylabel("Complete collector seconds (lower is faster)")\nax.set_title("100 owned local URLs: observed median\\n2 trials; 25ms fixture delay; 20,000 score rounds", fontsize=11)\nax.spines[["top", "right"]].set_visible(False)\nfig.text(0.5, 0.015, "Includes pool startup and shutdown. Synthetic local workload.", ha="center", fontsize=9)\nfig.tight_layout(rect=(0, 0.04, 1, 1))\nfig.savefig("chart.png", dpi=110)\nplt.close(fig)'),
  'Run a smaller report and reconstruct both recorded count and score checksum from results.csv.',
  G('import csv\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom lab import write_report\nfolder = Path("small-artifacts")\nwrite_report(folder, count=8, repeats=1, delay=0.001, rounds=100, cpu_rounds=100)\nwith (folder / "results.csv").open(encoding="utf-8", newline="") as handle:\n    rows = list(csv.DictReader(handle))\nreport = json.loads((folder / "report.json").read_text(encoding="utf-8"))\nprint("Count reconstructed:", len(rows) == report["result_count"] == 8)\nprint("Checksum reconstructed:", sum(int(row["score"]) for row in rows) == report["result_checksum"])'),
  'Preserve measured samples for all methods and check that every recorded ratio agrees with its medians.',
  G('from lab import write_report\nreport = write_report("tiny-artifacts", count=4, repeats=1, delay=0.001, rounds=100, cpu_rounds=100)\nsamples = report["collection_samples_seconds"]\nmedians = report["collection_median_seconds"]\nratios = report["observed_ratio_to_sequential"]\nprint("One retained trial per method:", all(len(values) == 1 for values in samples.values()))\nprint("Ratios reconstruct:", all(abs(ratios[name] - medians["sequential"] / value) < 1e-12 for name, value in medians.items()))\nprint("CPU methods:", sorted(report["cpu_experiment"]["median_seconds"]))\nprint("No speedup is required for a correct experiment")'),
  [('What if the measured process method is slower on small jobs?', 'Report it and investigate startup/transfer/workload costs', 'Delete its timing', 'Change the output until it looks faster', 'Correct measurement can disprove the expected performance advantage.'),
   ('Does matching count and score-sum prove cryptographic authenticity?', 'No', 'Always', 'Only for CSV', 'These checks establish simple internal consistency, not tamper-proof provenance.')],
  'Publish a speedup based on fewer successful jobs, omitted startup or discarded slower trials.', 'Retain complete equal results, raw samples, declared scope and all measured methods.',
  'The chart is generated from this actual run. Its measured numbers may differ on your computer; correctness, boundaries and honest interpretation are the durable lessons.',
  syntax='python -m pytest -q\npython lab.py --output artifacts\n# Inspect results.csv and report.json before drawing performance conclusions.',
  shell='python -m pip install -r requirements-authoring.txt\npython -m pytest -q\npython lab.py --output artifacts',
  figure=('Measured whole-collector times for 100 owned local URLs', 'Actual authoring-run medians of two trials. All collectors returned equal complete scored results. Pool startup/transfers/shutdown are included; the shared fixture startup is outside the timed calls. Local synthetic timings are not a public-web performance guarantee.'))
